# Directional overview and goals

I am looking to shorten and improve the quality of my draft essay.

The well-known quote "If I had more time, I would have written a shorter letter" applies to my case. My first draft is closer to a brain dump than a well-crafted piece of writing that someone else would find interesting and enjoyable to read. I'm also constrained by a maximum character limit. The essay needs to be cut by about 30% at least.

I want to optimize the essay to focus on two things:
- clear, concise communication of the main ideas and important points people will remember: the things that would appear in the "CliffsNotes summary" should be the main focus;
- sections that are engaging to read, like original ideas and interesting metaphors.

Anything that doesn't fit into the two categories above, like dry discussion or detailed technical deep dives, is a prime candidate for serious compression or removal. For things that do make the cut, I want the writing style to really be clear and concise, removing extraneous details, overly wordy phrasing, and unnecessary idea repetition.

